  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › How is the present simple formed?</p>
  <h1>How is the present simple formed in English?</h1>
  <p class="lede">The present simple is formed with the base form of the verb — with one change: in the third person singular (he, she, it), you add -s or -es to the verb. So “I work”, “you work”, “we work” and “they work” all use “work”, but “he works”, “she works” and “it works” add -s. That is the whole secret of the present simple.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>The basic form: he, she, it + -s</h2>
  <p>Most verbs simply add -s: work → works, live → lives, play → plays, read → reads. For example: “She works in a hospital”, “He lives in London”, “It rains a lot here.”</p>
  <p>A few spelling rules apply. Verbs ending in -s, -ss, -sh, -ch, -x or -o add -es: watch → watches, wash → washes, go → goes, do → does. Verbs ending in a consonant + y change y to -ies: study → studies, try → tries, carry → carries. But verbs ending in a vowel + y just add -s: play → plays, enjoy → enjoys.</p>
  <h2>Questions: do and does</h2>
  <p>To ask a question in the present simple, use do or does before the subject — and the main verb goes back to its base form, without -s. “Do you speak English?” (not “Do you speaks English?”), “Does she work here?” (not “Does she works here?”).</p>
  <p>Use “do” with I, you, we and they; use “does” with he, she and it. Short answers repeat the same word: “Do you like coffee?” — “Yes, I do.” / “No, I don’t.” “Does he live here?” — “Yes, he does.”</p>
  <h2>Negatives: don’t and doesn’t</h2>
  <p>To make a negative, use don’t or doesn’t before the verb — again, the main verb stays in its base form. “I don’t like tea”, “She doesn’t speak French”, “They don’t work on Sundays.” A very common mistake is “He don’t like it” — with he, she and it, it must be “doesn’t”: “He doesn’t like it.”</p>
  <p>Don’t is short for “do not”, and doesn’t is short for “does not”. In speaking, almost everyone uses the short forms.</p>
  <h2>When do we use the present simple?</h2>
  <p>The present simple is used for four main things. First, habits and routines: “I get up at 7 o’clock”, “She plays tennis on Sundays.” Second, facts and general truths: “Water boils at 100 degrees”, “The sun rises in the east.” Third, timetables and schedules: “The train leaves at 9:30”, “The film starts at 8.” Fourth, likes, dislikes and opinions: “I love music”, “He hates cold weather.”</p>
  <p>Notice that the present simple is not normally used for something happening right now — that is the present continuous: “I am working now”, not “I work now”. This is one of the most common mistakes learners make.</p>
  <h2>Present simple vs present continuous</h2>
  <p>Use the present simple for things that are generally true or repeated: “I speak English”, “She works in a shop.” Use the present continuous for things happening at this moment or around now: “I am speaking English right now”, “She is working today.”</p>
  <p>Some verbs are almost never used in the continuous — like, love, hate, know, want, need, understand. We say “I like coffee”, not “I am liking coffee”. Learning this difference early will make your English sound much more natural.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Forgetting the -s: “He work here” should be “He works here”.</li>
    <li>Keeping the -s in questions and negatives: “Does she works?” should be “Does she work?”</li>
    <li>Using “do” with he, she or it: “Does he lives here?” should be “Does he live here?”</li>
    <li>Using the present simple for something happening now: “I work now” should be “I am working now”.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Practise the -s ending out loud: he works, she lives, it rains — say them until they are automatic.</li>
    <li>Learn the three spelling rules: -es after s/sh/ch/x/o, and -ies after consonant + y.</li>
    <li>When you ask a question, remember: does + base verb, never does + -s verb.</li>
    <li>Write five sentences about your daily routine using the present simple, then read them aloud.</li>
  </ul>

