  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How is the present simple formed?</summary><p>Take the base form of the verb. For I, you, we and they, that is the whole form: “I work”, “they live”. For he, she and it, add -s or -es: “she works”, “he watches”, “it goes”. Questions use do or does, and negatives use don’t or doesn’t, with the base verb.</p></details>
  <details><summary>When do we use the present simple?</summary><p>For habits and routines (“I get up at 7”), facts (“Water boils at 100 degrees”), timetables (“The train leaves at 9:30”) and opinions (“I love music”). For something happening right now, use the present continuous instead (“I am working now”).</p></details>
  <details><summary>Why do we add -s to verbs with he, she and it?</summary><p>English verbs change only in the third person singular in the present simple. It is one of the few places where English verbs change their form, so it stands out — and it is also one of the most common mistakes learners make. Practising it aloud is the fastest way to make it automatic.</p></details>
